===Etymology 2===
According to Pfeifer: Originally from the coast in northern Germany with the meaning “any obstruction in water built with posts etc.”, from {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{term|wer|lang=gmh}} from {{etyl|goh|de}} {{term|wer|lang=goh}}, both related to the precursor of ''[[wehren]]'' and cognate with Old English [[wer]], English [[weir]], Dutch [[weer]].<ref>http://www.dwds.de/?qu=Wehr&hidx=2</ref> 

According to Duden: Either identical to [[Wehr#Noun|die Wehr]] (in which case the original meaning was “[[fortification]] against water”) or in the sense of “[[wickerwork]]”, as in the etymology of [[wehren]] (in which case the original meaning was “fish [[weir]]”).<ref>http://www.duden.de/rechtschreibung/Wehr_Anlage_Einrichtung_Stauwehr</ref>   


